answer.text:
**Student A is correct.**

For a MOSFET operating above threshold in saturation,

\[
I_D=\frac12 \mu_n C_{ox}\frac{W}{L}V_{ov}^2,\qquad V_{ov}=V_{GS}-V_{TH}
\]

so the transconductance is

\[
g_m=\frac{\partial I_D}{\partial V_{GS}}
=\mu_n C_{ox}\frac{W}{L}V_{ov}
=\frac{2I_D}{V_{ov}}
=\sqrt{2\mu_n C_{ox}\frac{W}{L}I_D}.
\]

Thus, at fixed device dimensions, \(g_m\) is directly proportional to the overdrive voltage (\(g_m\propto V_{ov}\)). As \(V_{ov}\) increases, \(g_m\) increases (linearly in the long-channel square-law model; sub-linearly but still upward in short-channel devices with mobility degradation/velocity saturation). Student B is incorrect.

Student A is correct in the usual saturation-region case for a fixed MOSFET:

\[
I_D=\frac12 \mu C_{ox}\frac WL V_{ov}^2,\qquad 
g_m=\frac{\partial I_D}{\partial V_{GS}}
=\mu C_{ox}\frac WL V_{ov}
=\frac{2I_D}{V_{ov}}
\]

where \(V_{ov}=V_{GS}-V_{th}\). So if \(V_{ov}\) increases by raising \(V_{GS}\) for the same transistor, \(g_m\) increases linearly with \(V_{ov}\) (equivalently, \(g_m\propto \sqrt{I_D}\)).

Caveat: if drain current is held fixed while \(V_{ov}\) is changed by sizing/\(V_{th}\), then \(g_m=2I_D/V_{ov}\) decreases as \(V_{ov}\) increases. That is a different constraint.
